<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">What this lesson adds</h2>
<p>The first lesson asked about one event at a time, such as <em>X</em> = 4 for a die roll. This lesson asks what happens when <strong>two or more events are considered together</strong>. If we roll two dice, &ldquo;the first die shows 3 <strong>and</strong> the second die shows 5&rdquo; combines two events. In a medical example, let <em>C</em> record whether the patient has the condition and let <em>T</em> record the test result. We can then ask, &ldquo;What is the probability that the patient has the condition (<em>C</em> = 1) <strong>and</strong> the test is positive (<em>T</em> = positive)?&rdquo; This question combines two events.</p>
<p>A generated word can be broken into several events as well. Let <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> be the first letter, <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> the second letter, and so on. Producing <strong>hello</strong> means that all five of these events occur:</p>
<p style="text-align: center; font-size: 1.1rem;"><em>L</em><sub>1</sub> = h <strong>AND</strong> <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> = e <strong>AND</strong> <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> = l <strong>AND</strong> <em>L</em><sub>4</sub> = l <strong>AND</strong> <em>L</em><sub>5</sub> = o.</p>
<p>The probability of that complete combination is a <strong>joint probability</strong>. When the model chooses each next letter using the letters already produced, it uses <strong>conditional probability</strong>: for example, the probability of e given that the first letter was h. The important change from Lesson 1 is not merely that one variable has more possible values; it is that we are asking about several events together.</p>
