<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">A complete three-letter example</h2>
<p>To make the three kinds of probability concrete, suppose a tiny text generator can produce exactly one of the eight three-letter words below. Let <em>L</em><sub>1</sub> be the first letter, <em>L</em><sub>2</sub> the second letter, and <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> the third letter. The probabilities are invented for this example, but they obey an essential rule: the probabilities of all eight possible outputs add to 1.</p>
<p><strong>Notation policy:</strong> Some texts reserve lowercase <em>p</em> for a probability density. From this point forward, this course will use lowercase <em>p</em> more broadly for both discrete probability mass functions and continuous probability density functions. This is a deliberate convention, not a claim that mass and density are the same thing. Use the variable and its possible values to determine which meaning applies.</p>
<ul>
<li>If the variable has separate possibilities&mdash;such as letters, tokens, or categories&mdash;<em>p</em> means <strong>probability mass</strong>. The masses assigned to all possible outcomes sum to 1.</li>
<li>If the variable can vary continuously&mdash;such as a coordinate or measurement&mdash;<em>p</em> means <strong>probability density</strong>. A density value is not itself a probability; probabilities come from the area under the density over an interval or region.</li>
</ul>
<p>Here, <em>L</em><sub>1</sub>, <em>L</em><sub>2</sub>, and <em>L</em><sub>3</sub> take discrete letter values. Therefore, <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline" aria-label="p of l one comma l two comma l three"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> means the joint probability mass assigned to one particular three-letter combination.</p>
