<p>Within the b group, the conditional probabilities 0.72 and 0.28 add to 1. Within the c group, 0.86 and 0.14 add to 1. Conditioning creates a new probability distribution over the smaller group of possibilities that remain after the condition is known.</p>
<p>This is the same operation a text generator performs repeatedly. After the model has generated b, it assigns probabilities to possible second letters. The known context can also be an entire prompt: given the prompt <strong>Complete the sentence with one word: Two plus two is &hellip;</strong>, the model assigns probabilities to possible next tokens such as <strong>four</strong>.</p>
<p>&nbsp;</p>
<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">How the calculations work</h2>
<p>A joint-probability table lists the probability of every combination. In Equation (2.1), lowercase <em>x</em> and <em>y</em> stand for particular values of the two random variables&mdash;for example, two particular tokens. To find a marginal probability, add the entries that share the result you care about. The &sum; symbol is <strong>sigma</strong>; as in Lesson 1, it means &ldquo;add the indicated terms.&rdquo; To find a conditional probability, keep only the group named after &ldquo;given,&rdquo; then calculate the fraction of that group with the result you want.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 2.1. The joint mass is p of x comma y. The marginal p of x is found by summing p of x comma y over every y. The conditional p of y given x is the joint mass divided by p of x."><mtable columnalign="right left" rowspacing="0.35em"><mtr><mtd><mtext>joint:</mtext></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>marginal:</mtext></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mo>&sum;</mo><mi>y</mi></munder><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mtext>conditional:</mtext></mtd><mtd><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(2.1)</span></div>
<p>The worked example above used three letter variables. Equation (2.1) uses only two variables, <em>x</em> and <em>y</em>, to keep the general rule compact. The same rule produced the table result <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline" aria-label="the probability that L two equals a given L one equals b"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">a</mi><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>L</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math> = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="inline" aria-label="zero point three six divided by zero point five zero equals zero point seven two"><mrow><mfrac><mn>0.36</mn><mn>0.50</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0.72</mn></mrow></math>: the numerator is the joint probability of the rows satisfying both requirements, and the denominator is the marginal probability of the known condition.</p>
<p>If you multiply both sides of the conditional-probability formula by <em>p(x)</em>, you get the <strong>product rule</strong>. It gives two equivalent ways to calculate the same joint probability.</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 2.2. The joint mass p of x comma y equals p of y given x times p of x, and also equals p of x given y times p of y."><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(2.2)</span></div>
<h2 class="content-box pad-box-mini" style="background-color: #941728; color: #ffffff;">What Bayes&rsquo; theorem does</h2>
<p><strong>Bayes&rsquo; theorem is important because it provides a mathematical framework for learning from evidence.</strong> Here, &ldquo;learning&rdquo; means starting with an existing belief, observing new data, and updating that belief in a consistent way. It does not mean that the new belief must be correct; poor assumptions or misleading data can still lead us astray. Bayes&rsquo; theorem tells us how the evidence should change the probabilities we assign.</p>
<p>For a concrete example, imagine that two AI systems, Model A and Model B, can generate short answers. You receive an answer but are not told which model produced it. Suppose you know how often Model A uses a particular phrase. After you notice that phrase in the answer you received, you want to ask: <strong>Given that I saw this phrase, how likely is it that Model A generated the answer?</strong> Bayes&rsquo; theorem connects what you knew before seeing the phrase with the evidence supplied by the phrase. In Equation (2.3), <em>h</em> is the hypothesis &ldquo;Model A generated this answer,&rdquo; and <em>e</em> is the evidence &ldquo;the answer contains this phrase.&rdquo;</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 2.3. The probability of hypothesis h given evidence e equals the probability of e given h, multiplied by the prior probability of h, and divided by the total probability of e."><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>|</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(2.3)</span></div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Prior:</strong> what you believed about the hypothesis before seeing the new evidence.</li>
<li><strong>Likelihood:</strong> how probable the evidence would be if the hypothesis were true.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence:</strong> the total probability of seeing that evidence across all competing possibilities.</li>
<li><strong>Posterior:</strong> the updated probability of the hypothesis after using the evidence.</li>
</ul>
<p>The denominator is not decorative: it accounts for every way the evidence could occur and makes the updated probabilities add to one.</p>
<h3>Work the Model A example</h3>
<p>Now calculate the probability instead of stopping at the vocabulary. Imagine 1,000 generated answers. Model A produced 100 of them, while other models produced the remaining 900. The phrase appears in 80 answers from Model A and in 90 answers from the other models.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Prior:</strong> Model A produced 100 of the 1,000 answers, so <em>p</em>(A) = 100/1000 = 0.10.</li>
<li><strong>Likelihood:</strong> The phrase appears in 80 of the 100 answers from Model A, so <em>p</em>(phrase | A) = 80/100 = 0.80.</li>
<li><strong>Evidence:</strong> The phrase appears in 80 + 90 = 170 of all 1,000 answers, so <em>p</em>(phrase) = 170/1000 = 0.17.</li>
</ul>
<p>Bayes&rsquo; theorem asks what fraction of the phrase-containing answers came from Model A:</p>
<div style="display: flex; align-items: center; margin: 0.75rem 0;">
<div style="flex: 1 1 auto; min-width: 0; text-align: center; overflow-x: auto;"><math style="font-size: 1.2rem;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block" aria-label="Equation 2.4. The total probability of seeing the phrase is eighty plus ninety divided by one thousand, which equals zero point one seven. The probability that Model A generated the answer given that the phrase appears equals the probability of the phrase given Model A times the prior probability of Model A, divided by the total probability of the phrase. Substituting the numbers gives zero point eight times zero point one divided by zero point one seven, which is approximately zero point four seven."><mtable columnalign="right left" rowspacing="0.65em"><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>phrase</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>90</mn></mrow><mn>1000</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>170</mn><mn>1000</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0.17</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>|</mo><mtext>phrase</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>phrase</mtext><mo>|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mtext>phrase</mtext><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd></mtd><mtd><mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0.80</mn><mo>&times;</mo><mn>0.10</mn></mrow><mn>0.17</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>0.08</mn><mn>0.17</mn></mfrac><mo>&asymp;</mo><mn>0.47</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math></div>
<span style="width: 3.5rem; flex: 0 0 3.5rem; text-align: right;" aria-hidden="true">(2.4)</span></div>
<p>The posterior probability is therefore about 0.47, or 47%. This is also visible directly in the counts: among the 170 answers containing the phrase, 80 came from Model A, and 80/170 is about 47%.</p>
<p>Notice why the answer is not 80%. The value 80% is the <strong>likelihood</strong>: the phrase is common when Model A generates an answer. The value 47% is the <strong>posterior</strong>: after seeing the phrase, it is our updated probability that Model A generated this particular answer. Those probabilities answer different questions.</p>
<p>In generative AI, a conditional model represents a distribution such as &ldquo;possible outputs given this prompt.&rdquo; The prompt supplies known information and changes which outputs are more or less likely.</p>
