<h3>Demo 4: Autoregressive Word Generation</h3>
<p>This demonstration shows how joint and conditional probabilities work in autoregressive generation—the same pattern language models use to generate text token by token.</p>
<p>The program starts with an empty string and generates a five-letter word one letter at a time. At each step, it:</p>
<ul>
<li>Shows <strong>P(context)</strong>—the marginal probability of the letters generated so far</li>
<li>Computes <strong>P(next | context)</strong>—the conditional probability of each possible next letter</li>
<li>Samples from that conditional distribution</li>
<li>Shows <strong>P(context + sampled)</strong>—the joint probability after adding the new letter</li>
</ul>
<p>The program works from a list of 163 common five-letter English words. Each conditional distribution shows <em>all</em> possible next letters sorted by probability, so you can see the full distribution the model samples from—just like an LLM choosing its next token.</p>

<p><strong>Try one change:</strong> Run the program multiple times. Because the random seed is set to <code>None</code>, each run produces variety—different generated words show different probability distributions. Notice how P(next | context) becomes more peaked (concentrated on fewer letters) as the context grows. Press 'q' to quit early if you want to restart and see a different generation path.</p>

<p><strong>What to observe:</strong> Watch how the conditional probabilities change at each step. After generating 't', for example, the probability of 'h' as the next letter becomes very high because many English words begin with 'th'. This is how context shapes generation in autoregressive models.</p>

